	&lt;li&gt;In the given sentence&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;, &amp;lsquo;will&amp;rsquo; is wrongly used instead of &amp;lsquo;would&amp;rsquo; in the main clause.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;So, the correct sentence is:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;If I were&amp;nbsp;the Prime Minister&amp;nbsp;of Britain,&lt;b&gt; &lt;/b&gt;I &lt;strong&gt;would&amp;nbsp;pass&lt;/strong&gt; a bill for the free education and tax-free employment&amp;nbsp;for Indian students trying to learn and earn to make a living abroad&amp;nbsp;&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;38px&quot; src=&quot;//storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/21/12/5fda3740a3d00463c91b990f_16397363619933.png&quot; width=&quot;160px&quot; /&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is important to remember that &amp;lsquo;were&amp;rsquo; and not &amp;lsquo;was&amp;rsquo; is used after the subject in the subordinate clause to make an imaginary or hypothetical statement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Let&amp;#39;s see an example-
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;If I&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;were&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;the President of America, I would make higher education in America free.&amp;nbsp;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/21/12/5fda3740a3d00463c91b990f_16397363619944.png&quot; style=&quot;height: 25px; width: 25px;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;If I&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;was&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;the President of America, I would make higher education in America free.&amp;nbsp;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/21/12/5fda3740a3d00463c91b990f_16397363619945.png&quot; style=&quot;height: 25px; width: 25px;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;Option 3 i.e., &amp;lsquo;we will have had&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;&amp;rsquo;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;- the error lies in this part only.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/images/production/quesImages/quesImage56.png&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;This is a&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;conditional sentence,&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;and in this type of sentence,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;one sentence depends upon the other because of cause and effect.&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;In this case, this type of sentence follows a special structure.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;Conditional words&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;are&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;If&lt;/u&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;until&lt;/u&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;unless&lt;/u&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;as soon as&lt;/u&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;when&lt;/u&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;while&lt;/u&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;as long as&lt;/u&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;since&lt;/u&gt;, etc.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;table border=&quot;1&quot; cellpadding=&quot;1&quot; cellspacing=&quot;1&quot; style=&quot;background-color: (255, 255, 255); width: 450px;&quot;&gt;
	&lt;tbody&gt;
		&lt;tr&gt;
			&lt;td&gt;
			&lt;ol&gt;
				&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;Conditional word + subject + v1/v5 + object, subject + will/ shall/ may + v1 + object.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
				&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;If + subject + v2 + object, subject + would/ should/ could/ might + v1 + object.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
				&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;If + subject + had + v3 + object, subject + would/ should/ could/ might + have + v3 + object.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
				&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;Had + subject + v3 + object, subject + would + have + v3 + object.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
			&lt;/ol&gt;
			&lt;/td&gt;
		&lt;/tr&gt;
	&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;The given sentence follows rule number &amp;lsquo;4&amp;rsquo;, and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;according to the rule&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;, the error lies in Part &amp;lsquo;C&amp;rsquo;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/images/production/quesImages/quesImage265.png&quot; /&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;For example-&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;Had John learned driving, he&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;would have landed&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;a job as a trainer in the driving school.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;In the given sentence,&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;&amp;lsquo;will&amp;rsquo; is wrongly used instead of &amp;lsquo;would&amp;rsquo; before &amp;lsquo;have&amp;rsquo; in the main clause.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hence, &amp;lsquo;will have had&amp;rsquo; should be replaced by &amp;lsquo;would have had&amp;rsquo;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;So, the correct sentence is:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Had M.S. Dhoni&amp;nbsp;led&amp;nbsp;us&amp;nbsp;in the T20 World Cup Championship 2019,&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;we would&amp;nbsp;have had&amp;nbsp;two&amp;nbsp;World Cups with us today.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/images/production/quesImage7.png&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/images/production/quesImages/quesImage3209.png&quot; /&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;Mistake Point:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is important to remember that if the conditional clause starts with &amp;lsquo;had&amp;rsquo;, it is followed by &amp;lsquo;would + have + v3&amp;rsquo; in the main clause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;For example-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;diams;Had we reached the theatre on time, we&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;would have seen&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;Robert Pattinson.&amp;nbsp;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/images/production/quesImage7.png&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;diams;Had we reached the theatre on time, we&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;will have seen&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;Robert Pattinson.&amp;nbsp;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//cdn.testbook.com/images/production/quesImages/quesImage374.png&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The least possible Venn diagram for the given statements is as follows:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/21/02/F1_Sagar_Shraddha_01.02.2021_%20Correction%20_D9.png&quot; style=&quot;width: 294px; height: 125px;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I. No bed is blanket &amp;rarr; True (as all bed are part of sheet and none of the sheet is part of blanket)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;II. Some bed are pillow &amp;rarr; False (It is possible but not&amp;nbsp;definite)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hence,&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Only conclusion I follows.&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

